<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n44012" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">sessor</span></b> , ōris, m.
		sedeo, <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b><i>one who sits</i> in a place, <i>a sitter</i>
			(not ante-Aug. and very rare). </div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b> In gen., <i>a sitter in the theatre</i>,
					<i><span class="en">Hor. Ep. 2, 2,
			130</span></i>.—</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense3"><b>2.</b>
			<i>A sitter upon a horse</i>, <i>a
				horseman</i>, <i>rider</i>, <i><span class="en">Sen.
				Const. 12 <i>fin.</i></span></i>; <i><span class="en">Suet.
				Caes. 61</span></i>; <i><span class="en">Veg. 2, 28,
			34</span></i>.—* </div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>II.</b> In partic., <i>one who tarries</i> or <i>dwells</i> in a place, <i>an
				inhabitant</i>, <i>resident</i>: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">sessores veteres urbis,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Nep.
					Cim. 2, 5</span></i>
			.</div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
    <div class="alpheios-lex-alph-source">From <u>
            <u>A Latin Dictionary</u>
        </u> (Charlton T. Lewis, Charles Short, Oxford, Clarendon Press, 1879) </div>
